<h1>AI: Segmentation</h1>
<p class="lead">Why this matters: object detection (Lesson 0022) draws boxes around objects. Segmentation goes a level deeper — it labels every pixel. For alt-data pipelines like crop NDVI (Lesson 0020) or oil-tank fill detection (Lesson 0015), segmentation is the natural tool because the question is "what is this pixel?" not "where is this object?"</p>

<h2>1. The three segmentation tasks</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Task</th><th>Output</th><th>Distinguishes individual instances?</th></tr>
<tr><td>Semantic segmentation</td><td>Every pixel gets a class label.</td><td>No. All "car" pixels are the same class.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Instance segmentation</td><td>Every pixel gets a class + instance ID.</td><td>Yes. "Car 1" and "Car 2" are separate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Panoptic segmentation</td><td>Combines the two. "Things" (countable: cars, ships) get instance IDs; "stuff" (uncountable: sky, road, water) gets only a class.</td><td>For things, yes; for stuff, no.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2. Why segmentation for alt-data</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>Crop NDVI (Lesson 0020)</h4><p>You want the NDVI <em>only over crop pixels</em>, not over roads, buildings, or water. Semantic segmentation produces a crop mask which you then apply to the NDVI map.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Oil-tank fill detection</h4><p>The tank's outline is needed to measure the shadow length of the floating roof. Semantic segmentation gives the tank's boundary; analysis runs on the bounded region.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Port activity</h4><p>Counting ships needs <em>instance</em> segmentation — touching ships need to be distinguished from one another. Box-based detection alone fails when ships are docked side-by-side.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Building footprint extraction</h4><p>Urbanization signals for property markets. Semantic seg gives building/non-building pixels; instance seg gives individual buildings.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>3. The model families</h2>

<h3>(a) U-Net (2015): the OG semantic segmentation architecture</h3>
<p>An encoder-decoder convnet with skip connections between corresponding encoder and decoder layers. The encoder downsamples (gathering context); the decoder upsamples (recovering resolution); skip connections preserve fine detail. Still a strong baseline for satellite imagery; trivially small to train.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup></p>

<h3>(b) Mask R-CNN (2017): instance segmentation by extension</h3>
<p>Take Faster R-CNN's two-stage detection, add a mask-prediction head on top of each detected box. Outputs per-instance pixel masks. Slower than YOLO but the standard baseline for instance segmentation in research.</p>

<h3>(c) SAM — Segment Anything Model (Meta, 2023)</h3>
<p>A foundation model for segmentation. Trained on 11M images with 1.1B masks. Given a prompt (a point, a box, or text), SAM produces a high-quality mask for the indicated region — without per-domain fine-tuning.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<p>SAM is a step-change for alt-data:</p>
<ul>
<li>You can prompt with a point ("segment whatever's here") and SAM produces a mask.</li>
<li>Combine with Grounding DINO (Lesson 0022) for text-driven prompts → fully open-vocabulary instance segmentation.</li>
<li>Fine-tuning is optional for many tasks; it works zero-shot.</li>
</ul>

<h3>(d) Transformer-based (Mask2Former, SegFormer)</h3>
<p>Adopt the DETR-style set-prediction approach for segmentation. Strong on panoptic tasks; competitive with CNNs in 2024–25.</p>

<h2>4. The standard output</h2>
<pre><span class="com"># Semantic segmentation output: a 2D array same size as the input image,</span>
<span class="com"># each cell holds an integer class label.</span>
mask = model(image)            <span class="com"># shape: (H, W), values in [0, num_classes-1]</span>

<span class="com"># Instance segmentation output: a list of (binary_mask, class_id, confidence)</span>
instances = model(image)
<span class="kw">for</span> inst <span class="kw">in</span> instances:
    binary_mask = inst[<span class="str">"mask"</span>]   <span class="com"># shape: (H, W), boolean</span>
    class_id    = inst[<span class="str">"cls"</span>]
    score       = inst[<span class="str">"score"</span>]
</pre>

<h2>5. Evaluation metrics</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Definition</th><th>Use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pixel accuracy</td><td>Fraction of pixels classified correctly.</td><td>Bad for imbalanced classes (background dominates).</td></tr>
<tr><td>mIoU (mean IoU)</td><td>Mean of IoU across all classes.</td><td>Headline metric for semantic segmentation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dice coefficient</td><td>2·|A∩B| / (|A|+|B|). Equivalent to F1 for binary masks.</td><td>Common in medical imaging; also fine for satellite.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AP@mask</td><td>Average precision computed on mask-IoU rather than box-IoU.</td><td>Instance segmentation benchmark.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6. Illustrative pipeline: oil-tank fill from satellite</h2>
<pre><span class="com"># 1. Detect tanks (box-level) with a YOLO-family model.</span>
boxes = yolo(satellite_tile, classes=[<span class="str">"oil_tank"</span>])

<span class="kw">for</span> box <span class="kw">in</span> boxes:
    <span class="com"># 2. Crop to the tank box, run SAM with the box as prompt → fine mask.</span>
    tank_crop = <span class="fn">crop</span>(satellite_tile, box)
    mask      = <span class="fn">sam</span>(tank_crop, prompt=box)

    <span class="com"># 3. Find the floating-roof shadow inside the tank mask.</span>
    shadow_mask = <span class="fn">detect_shadow</span>(tank_crop, mask)

    <span class="com"># 4. Convert shadow length + tank diameter + sun angle → roof height → fill level.</span>
    fill_pct = <span class="fn">geometry</span>(shadow_mask, box.w, sun_elevation)
    <span class="fn">emit</span>(box.center_latlon, fill_pct)
</pre>
<p>That's the structure of one of the most lucrative alt-data feeds in the industry — Ursa Space's oil-tank inventory product (Lesson 0015) is a polished production version of this pattern.</p>

<h2>7. Often confused with</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>Semantic vs Instance segmentation</h4><p><strong>Semantic</strong>: all "car" pixels look identical in the output. <strong>Instance</strong>: each individual car has its own ID. Counting requires instance.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Panoptic vs Instance</h4><p><strong>Panoptic</strong>: includes "stuff" classes (sky, road) for which instance IDs make no sense. <strong>Instance</strong>: typically only outputs masks for the things you care about, ignoring backgrounds.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>SAM vs U-Net</h4><p><strong>SAM</strong>: prompt-driven foundation model, zero-shot for many tasks. <strong>U-Net</strong>: train per task, much smaller, deployable on edge devices.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Mask R-CNN vs YOLO-seg</h4><p>Both do instance segmentation. <strong>Mask R-CNN</strong>: two-stage, more accurate, slower. <strong>YOLO-seg</strong> (v8/v11): single-stage, fast, real-time. YOLO-seg has largely supplanted Mask R-CNN in production.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>8. Practice</h2>
<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q1. You need to count ships in a satellite image of a port. Two ships are docked side-by-side. Which task is the minimum required?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Semantic segmentation would label both ships as 'ship' pixels with no way to count them as two — they'd merge into one connected region.">Semantic segmentation.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Side-by-side ships in semantic seg are one connected component — uncountable. Instance segmentation gives each ship its own mask + instance ID, which lets you count them as 2 distinct objects.">Instance segmentation.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Classification gives one label for the whole image; can't count ships at all.">Image classification.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Object detection (bounding boxes) works if the ships are clearly separated, but adjacent docked ships often produce overlapping boxes that NMS may incorrectly suppress. Instance segmentation is more robust here.">Object detection with NMS.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q2. SAM (Segment Anything Model) is described as a "foundation model for segmentation." What does that mean operationally?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. SAM was pretrained on 11M images with 1.1B masks. It produces high-quality masks for arbitrary prompts (points, boxes, text via Grounding DINO) on most natural-image domains without fine-tuning. For alt-data exploration this is enormous because it removes the per-domain training step.">It produces high-quality masks zero-shot or with simple prompts (point, box, text) across many domains without per-task training.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="No model achieves 100% accuracy on every domain. SAM has limitations on highly specialized imagery (SAR, ultrasound) where fine-tuning helps.">It achieves 100% accuracy on any imagery domain.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="SAM is computationally heavy and is typically deployed on GPUs in the cloud, not embedded devices.">It's so small it fits on embedded hardware.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="SAM is supervised on images with masks. It's not a self-supervised approach in the language-model sense.">It learns segmentation without any labeled data.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q3. For crop NDVI estimation, you need to mask out non-crop pixels (roads, buildings, water) before averaging NDVI. Which segmentation task is the right fit?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. You don't care about distinguishing individual crop fields from each other — only about whether a pixel IS or ISN'T crop. Semantic segmentation produces exactly that mask: crop / non-crop per pixel. Cheap to train; cheap to deploy.">Semantic segmentation — a binary crop/non-crop mask per pixel.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Instance segmentation distinguishes individual crop fields, which isn't needed for an NDVI average across the region. Semantic is sufficient and cheaper.">Instance segmentation of every individual field.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Object detection boxes would include some non-crop pixels (around the edges of each field), defeating the purpose of masking. Semantic seg gives pixel-precise masks.">Object detection with bounding boxes around each field.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Image-level classification doesn't tell you which pixels are crop — and that's exactly what you need.">Image classification: 'this image contains crops or it doesn't'.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q4. Why is pixel accuracy a misleading metric for satellite segmentation tasks?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Pixel accuracy is well-defined for satellite images at any resolution. The problem isn't resolution.">Satellite images are too high-resolution.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Most satellite images are dominated by background classes (forest, water, soil). A model that predicts everything as background gets 90%+ pixel accuracy without doing anything useful. mIoU (or Dice) is robust to imbalance because it computes per-class IoU and averages.">Background classes typically dominate, so a model that predicts everything as background scores ~90% pixel accuracy without learning anything. mIoU and Dice are robust to imbalance.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Pixel accuracy is what you'd want to measure for some segmentation tasks; it's just misleading when classes are imbalanced.">It doesn't measure what you actually want to measure.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Computation cost is irrelevant — accuracy is just summing correct predictions. Imbalance is the real issue.">It's too expensive to compute at scale.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<h2>9. Scenarios</h2>
<details class="scenario"><summary>S1. You build a SAM-based pipeline for cargo-container counting at a port. Throughput is too slow for daily updates over global ports. How do you accelerate?</summary>
<p>Two-stage cascade. Use a fast lightweight detector (YOLO) to box candidate ports/containers; only run SAM on the cropped boxes. SAM is computationally expensive at full image scale but cheap on small crops. Expected speedup: 10–100× depending on how empty the imagery is. Second-line speedup: use SAM's smaller variants (MobileSAM, FastSAM) which trade some mask quality for big speed wins.</p></details>

<details class="scenario"><summary>S2. Your panoptic segmentation model trained on aerial urban imagery shows excellent mIoU but the segmentation looks visually wrong on edges. What's wrong with the metric?</summary>
<p>mIoU is averaged across the whole mask area. A large object with a slightly wrong boundary loses very little IoU because the boundary pixels are a small fraction of the total. So the model can have great mIoU and ugly edges. Two fixes: (1) use the boundary-IoU metric explicitly, which penalizes boundary errors; (2) add a boundary-aware loss term during training (DiceBoundaryLoss, Focal-edge). For applications like building-footprint extraction the boundary IS the product, so this matters a lot.</p></details>

